From: James Daugherty <jhdaugh@garnet.msen.com>
Date: Tue, 3 May 1994 05:01:38 -0400 (EDT)
Subject: [#94-5-22] Filtering Techniques (filtering the flood)

My reason for being on the net is to scan for ideas I don't know about!  
I am less concerned about losing excellent posts I would like to read, 
but cover topics of which I am somewhat conversant.  I figure I can alway 
study the topic in detail some time in the future as long as I know about 
the topic.

Therefore, I would be more concerned that my filtering system was 
dogmatically restricting my gaze to that with which I hae a tendency
to agree, though I don't want miss excellent posts on familiar topics 
either. 

It seems to me that mailing lists are inherently problematic because the 
flood of messages can become too great.  Perhaps Newsgroups are the only 
real answer that doesn't risk the early deletion of the unfamiliar.

Are Newsgroups expensive to operate?  Can subscription fees be charged?  
It seems to me that subscription fees would eliminate most of the 
intentional noise that I see plaguing many of the existing "alt." Newsgroups.

I am new to Internet.  Perhaps the above is old hat to "old hands"?

